<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Policy enforcement for LLM agents has shifted from single-turn content moderation to compound, application-specific policies evaluated over multi-turn transcripts and tool-call trajectories. Fine-tuned guardrail models are efficient but degrade on compound policies, and while LLM-as-a-judge approaches achieve higher accuracy, they incur high inference cost and tend to produce unstable predictions. Neurosymbolic methods offer a natural alternative, decomposing the policy into narrow neural decisions composed under explicit logic. However, existing symbolic layers lack the required expressiveness and are optimized against fixed data rather than the policy itself. To address this, we introduce SCOPE, which compiles a policy into an executable program of neural predicates executed by small models, deterministic extractors, and code that composes their outputs. The program is optimized jointly with its training data, using adversarial generation of counterexamples that target where the program deviates from the policy. We evaluate SCOPE on a benchmark of real-world policy tasks against prompt optimization, task decomposition, and workflow generation methods, achieving the highest average accuracy across tasks and models.
Programs optimized with the weakest executor transfer to other models without further optimization. This allows them to be deployed in production on inexpensive models, where they reach accuracy comparable to frontier reasoning models.
Unlike these monolithic models, SCOPE produces substantially more stable predictions, and each verdict is interpretable through the predicate outputs that produced it. Additionally, we validate the key components of our method through an ablation study. SCOPE thus enables reliable, interpretable, and efficient policy enforcement at production scale.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="Pt1" class="ltx_part">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_part"></h2>

<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h3>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models are increasingly deployed to enforce the policies of the
organizations that run them, from content rules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>]</cite>
and regulatory obligations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> to application-specific constraints
on permissible system behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. As these applications
advance from single-turn moderation to multi-turn assistants and tool-using
agents, both the policies and the inputs they govern grow more complex. The unit
of judgment becomes a long transcript <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>]</cite>, a structured plan
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, or an agent trajectory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> rather than a single
message, and a modern policy is rarely one flat category: it bundles conditions,
exceptions, counts, and interactions that must be assessed together
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Adhering to such a policy is therefore both critical and
hard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>]</cite>, as the verdict turns on a compound, context-dependent specification rather
than a single surface feature, where one overlooked clause produces a false
violation or a missed obligation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The most direct approach uses a language model as the policy classifier itself.
Its principal advantage is generality: any policy that can be expressed in
natural language can be enforced without a predefined taxonomy
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>. This approach takes two forms. The first trains a custom
classifier on the task, typically a small fine-tuned guardrail model
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Such models are fast and
inexpensive, and on a single, well-scoped rule they match far larger models
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. Their capacity is the ceiling, so accuracy falls as a policy
compounds interacting conditions beyond what one model resolves in a
single pass <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. The second prompts a frontier model with the
policy at runtime and reads its verdict, the paradigm known as LLM-as-a-judge
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. While it is more capable on the compound case, its predictions are unstable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>, <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, with variance rising with the number of clauses in the
input <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>. Further, even dedicated reasoning models collapse past a complexity threshold rather than scaling through it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite>. Finally, its inference cost and latency make it impractical at production scale, where high-throughput classification is required. The first form is thus reliable only on simple policies, while the second is neither stable nor scalable.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.31062v1/overview_figure.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/202;" width="476" height="202" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(1) Given a natural-language policy and a small pool of seed samples, (2) the optimizer compiles the policy into (3) a decomposition program: predicates answered by weak models, deterministic extractors, and Python code that composes their outputs into a verdict. (4) Adversarial generators extend the training pool with counterexamples where the program departs from the policy, at the level of a single predicate (local) or the composition as a whole (systemic). The loop repeats, optimizing the program and its training data jointly.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A neurosymbolic approach is the natural alternative. Such methods pair neural
components that supply semantic judgment with a symbolic layer that composes
their outputs under explicit logic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>. Because each sub-decision is narrowly scoped and the composition is explicit, the verdict is more stable than a monolithic judgment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. As narrow sub-decisions are simpler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">102</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>, they can also be answered by small models, which makes the system cheaper to run. The weakness of existing methods lies in the symbolic layer itself, along two axes. The first is expressiveness: checklists <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, weighted rule sets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, and compiled Boolean or probabilistic rules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">93</a>]</cite> cannot express the conditional, iterative, and computational structure that complex real-world use cases carry. The second is bias: a learned or synthesized layer is fit to a proxy for the policy, not to the policy itself, whether a rule set tuned to its training samples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite>, a program searched against a static benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">99</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, or a function verified against self-generated tests and specifications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce SCOPE (Symbolic Composition Over Predicates and Extractors), which compiles a natural-language policy into a neurosymbolic program: neural predicates resolved by weak models, cheap sub-frontier models served without extended reasoning, deterministic extractors for exact computation, and executable code that composes their outputs. Expressiveness is met by the composition being code: it expresses the structure of real-world policies naturally, which rule-based formalisms capture only at low fidelity. Bias is mitigated by how the training data is built. Rather than relying only on a static training set, the search draws on an adversary that repeatedly generates counterexamples targeting where the current program departs from the specification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>, and the program is revised against them.
Counterexamples are produced by the adversary at two levels: local drift, where a predicate no longer tracks the clause it transcribes, and global drift, where the decomposition as a whole misses a predicate or misroutes an interaction between clauses. Each counterexample is labeled by a strong teacher applying the full policy, and the cost is paid once at compilation. The unit of optimization is thus not a single rule <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> but the compound policy: each round adds counterexamples and updates both the predicate formulations and the code that composes them. The converged program runs entirely on weak models, so it is cheap to serve. Each verdict is attributable to the predicate outputs behind it rather than a post-hoc rationale, and, being built from narrow classifications, is more stable than a monolithic judgment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We release a benchmark of four policy-enforcement tasks, derived from public benchmarks or modeled on deployed agentic systems. The tasks span long agent traces, multi-turn counting, critic auditing, and dynamic multi-label outputs. SCOPE attains the best accuracy in most task-executor settings, outperforming prompt optimization, neurosymbolic methods, and workflow generation. Its advantage grows as the executor weakens. Once a policy is compiled into predicates a weak model can answer without reasoning, accuracy becomes largely insensitive to the choice of executor. Programs compiled with our weakest executor transfer directly to other models, matching frontier reasoning models at a fraction of the inference cost. They are also far more stable: where reasoning models flip a substantial share of their verdicts across repeated runs, SCOPE’s predictions remain nearly fixed, and each verdict traces to the predicate outputs that produced it. Ablations confirm that adversarial generation is central to these gains. More broadly, SCOPE shows that natural-language policies can be compiled rather than interpreted, turning policy enforcement from an opaque judgment delegated to a frontier model into a program that can be optimized, inspected, and deployed at scale.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h3>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Policy enforcement.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Policy enforcement asks a classifier to decide whether an input satisfies a policy written in prose. Modern deployments impose requirements that earlier content moderation did not. Policies may involve complex combinations of conditions, exceptions, counts, and interactions that must be assessed together, potentially over long inputs such as multi-turn transcripts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>]</cite>, structured plans <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, or agent trajectories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, in which the evidence deciding the verdict occupies a small fraction of the context. On such a
policy the verdict must nonetheless be accurate, since one overlooked clause
yields a false violation or a missed obligation. It must also be
cheap, because the enforcer gates every interaction and its cost is paid on each
one <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite>, and consistent, because a verdict that
shifts with position or surface form is not an enforcement of the policy at all
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">86</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">96</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing approaches trade expressiveness against cost and consistency. Blocklists and
regular expressions are the least expressive: deterministic, perfectly
repeatable, and nearly free to run, but blind to anything their patterns do not
literally contain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>. Compact models fine-tuned on a single
narrow task judge semantics rather than surface form
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, and need no annotation once their
training data is synthesized from the policy text alone
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>; their capacity is the ceiling,
and accuracy falls once a policy bundles interacting conditions that a single forward pass must resolve together <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>. Guardrails conditioned on a policy supplied at runtime
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> are expressive and cheap, but, being far smaller than frontier models, they share the capacity ceiling above and still err on multi-clause policies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Frontier LLM-as-a-judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> is the most expressive and is accurate on compound policies. Yet it is the least consistent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite>, variance rising with the number of clauses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, and the most expensive, a frontier call for every gated interaction. Prompt optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">103</a>, <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>, <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>, <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> can tune the judge’s instructions, but as the verdict of a monolithic judge still comes from a single call, it inherits the same limitations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scope</span> breaks this trade-off. A policy is compiled into a program whose atoms
are predicates resolved by small models and whose composition is explicit code,
so the expressiveness of a frontier judge is obtained at the cost and the
repeatability of a weak classifier: no strong-model call is made at inference, the
composition is deterministic, and both the predicates and the code composing them
are optimized against the policy itself.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Neurosymbolic programs.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Neurosymbolic methods pair neural components, which supply semantic judgment,
with a symbolic layer that composes their outputs. Keeping the composition
explicit rather than folding it into a network’s weights makes the verdict
readable and checkable, and captures procedural structure that a single forward
pass does not <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Two axes organize what follows: how expressive the symbolic layer is, and how much of the method is learned rather than fixed in advance. On the first axis, the layer ranges from a logic program <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> to executable code <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, which, unlike a logic program whose rules are written by hand and whose neural predicates are trained through its inference, can be written and revised by an LLM and can call models directly on intermediate results.
On the second axis, both the composition and the neural predicates it calls may be either given or learned.
Classical differentiable programs fix the structure and train the predicates through it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, while others search for the structure as well: over a typed functional language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite>, guided by neural relaxations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite>, or over programs whose nodes invoke a language model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">99</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing methods have stayed on one side of each axis at a time. One line
derives a symbolic structure, generated from the policy or fit to data, but over a restricted formalism: a
count over atomic yes/no questions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, linear weights over
natural-language rules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite>, a probabilistic circuit over trajectory
predicates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, or a logical encoding checked by an SMT solver <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
Such a formalism is tractable to derive precisely because it is restricted, and for the same reason it cannot make which question is asked depend on earlier answers, count occurrences over spans extracted from the input, or repeat a check over parts of the input selected at run time, structures that real policies commonly contain.
The other line
writes the composition as code, gaining that expressiveness, but leaves the
program fixed: hand-authored rails <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>, a synthesized function
verified against self-generated tests and specifications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>]</cite>, or a program emitted
per task whose generator is never tuned against the policy
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>]</cite>. Its predicates are frozen prompts, so one
that drifts from the clause it transcribes stays drifted. SCOPE combines both lines: its composition is ordinary Python, and its structure and predicates are optimized jointly against the policy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Curriculum learning.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A training pool synthesized once from the task description
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">95</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> fixes the distribution
before optimization begins. Curriculum learning instead presents examples in a
deliberate order, from easy to hard rather than at random <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. The
order need not be set in advance: self-paced
learning selects the next examples by the model’s current competence
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, and automated curricula place a teacher in the loop that picks
each round’s examples to maximize the student’s learning progress
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>. Across these, the schedule is regenerated as the
model improves, so that training mass tracks where the model is currently weak
rather than the base rate of the data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One such curriculum builds its examples adversarially, generating rather than
ranking: instead of drawing from a fixed set, it produces the inputs on which the
current model fails <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. Human annotators write examples a target model gets wrong
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>; red-teaming automates that search with a
generator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>; and in program synthesis,
counterexample-guided loops propose a candidate, obtain inputs on which a verifier
reports failure, and fold them back in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scope</span> folds this adversarial curriculum into the decomposition search, building the training data and optimizing the program together. Unlike the schemes above, its target is a program with named sub-components, so a counterexample points to the part at fault, and its verifier is a strong model reading the policy rather than a sound solver, which sacrifices formal guarantees for applicability to policies no solver accepts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methods</h3>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SCOPE searches for an executable program <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> that reproduces the verdicts of a natural-language policy when every predicate is answered by a weak model. The policy assigns each input, such as a conversation or an agent trace, a label from a fixed set or, for multi-label tasks, a set of labels. As detailed in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 The Decomposition Program ‣ 3 Methods ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, the program consists of <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">predicates</em>, classification questions answered by a weak LLM, and <em id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">extractors</em>, deterministic functions over the input, combined by Python composition code. We denote by <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\pi,D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\pi,D)</annotation></semantics></math> the score of <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> on a pool <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, measured as accuracy for single-label tasks and the Jaccard index for multi-label tasks.
Only a small seed pool <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of human-labeled samples is given. During optimization, an adversarial process generates new samples that target where the program may depart from the policy and adds them to the pool. The program is thus fit to the policy rather than to a fixed dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates the optimization process. It starts from a single predicate, the policy itself, and alternates two components. The optimizer executes <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> on the current training pool <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, uses the predicate traces to attribute each failure to a predicate or to the composition code, and revises the program. The adversarial generators then produce samples on which the revised program is likely to disagree with the policy, targeting both individual predicates and the composition as a whole. A teacher applying the full policy labels them to form <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The search stops at a threshold <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> or after <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> rounds, and returns the program that scores highest on <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The search is carried out by strong LLM agents, whose prompts are given in Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Task decomposition prompts ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>. The predicates, by contrast, are part of the program and are answered by a weak model during the search. A program therefore scores well only if a weak model can execute it, matching the target deployment setting.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>The Decomposition Program</h4>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Structure.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A program consists of predicates, extractors, and composition code. A predicate is
a natural-language classification question with a fixed label set, typically
binary. An extractor is a deterministic function that returns a value, a span, or
a list of spans from the input. The composition code is a Python function that
invokes extractors to select spans, applies predicates to them, and maps the
resulting labels to a verdict. Predicates do not specify their input; the
composition code determines the text to which each predicate is applied. The same
predicate may therefore be applied to the full input, to a span within it, or
repeatedly within a single sample.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Expressing the composition as code allows the program to mirror the structure of the policy. A predicate can be restricted to the region its question concerns, such as one section of a long input, or applied multiple times to different parts of the input. Although predicate label sets are fixed, the program can handle tasks whose label set varies per sample or that require multiple labels, by determining how predicates are applied to the sample’s candidates and how their outputs form the final prediction. Predicates can also be invoked conditionally on earlier answers. Counts and thresholds can be computed exactly by deterministic extractors, rather than estimated by a model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Task decomposition example ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> presents a complete program.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During the search, every predicate call is answered by an <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">executor</em>, a sub-frontier model run with reasoning disabled and instructed to return only the label. The executor is chosen to match the capability of the models intended for deployment. A predicate that the executor cannot answer reliably is likely to be reworded or split into simpler questions. The structure of the program therefore adapts to the capability of the executor, and weaker executors yield programs with more predicates (Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>b). Since the executor receives only a predicate and its input, without the policy, each predicate must be self-contained, stating the definitions, scope, and exceptions of the clause it encodes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This choice of executor is motivated by cost, stability, and traceability. Sub-frontier models are considerably cheaper to run than frontier models, yet capable of answering narrowly scoped questions without any training. Running without reasoning is intended to keep predictions stable across runs (Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Cross-Executor Transferability ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>). Finally, executing a program on a sample produces a trace: the ordered sequence of predicate calls, each with the exact text it received and the label it returned. Traces make every verdict attributable to specific predicate outputs, and they are the primary signal for the search. The optimizer uses them to localize failures (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Optimizer ‣ 3 Methods ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), and the local adversary uses them to observe the inputs a predicate actually receives (Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Adversarial Generators ‣ 3 Methods ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Optimizer</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The search starts from the undecomposed task: a single predicate, the policy itself, applied to the full input. Before the first round, the teacher writes a rationale for each seed sample. At round <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the teacher labels the samples the adversaries added in the previous round (Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Adversarial Generators ‣ 3 Methods ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>), and the current program is executed on <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The analyzer receives the policy, the program, and, for every sample, the predicted verdict, the gold label, its rationale, and the trace. Supervision exists only for the final verdict, so the analyzer infers which calls caused a failure by working backward: it replays the composition code on the recorded predicate labels, finds the labels that would have had to differ to produce the gold verdict, and inspects the text those calls received. Each failure is thus attributed to a predicate whose wording does not encode its clause, or to composition code that misencodes the policy. Correctly classified samples with similar rationales serve as a contrast set for checking candidate fixes against regressions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The analyzer then recommends revisions: rewording or splitting a predicate, adding or removing a predicate or extractor, moving a condition between a predicate and an extractor, or rewriting the composition code. Each recommendation must be grounded in a clause, definition, or exception of the policy. A revision justified only by a phrase observed in a sample would fit the pool rather than the policy, and is excluded. The decomposer treats these recommendations as proposals and weighs them against the search history: previous programs and their scores, the revisions applied in the last round, and revisions already tried from the current program without improvement. When recent rounds do not improve the score, the search backtracks to the best program found so far, and the decomposer must pursue a substantially different direction, which prevents cycling among similar programs. The search terminates when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\pi,D_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\pi,D_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> reaches a threshold <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, or after <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> rounds. Since <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> grows across rounds, its scores are not comparable between rounds, so the returned program is the candidate with the highest score <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\pi,D_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\pi,D_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> on the fixed seed pool.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Adversarial Generators</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Optimizing against a fixed pool rewards fitting the pool: a policy condition that the pool rarely exercises can be omitted at little cost in accuracy, and the pool provides no signal to restore it. SCOPE therefore grows the pool adversarially. After each revision, two adversaries compare the policy with the current program and generate samples on which the two are likely to disagree. Supervision thus concentrates where the program is wrong, analogously to counterexample-guided synthesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>, with the policy as the specification. Both adversaries see the current pool, including <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and all previously generated samples, so new samples stay consistent with it and avoid redundancy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The adversaries operate at two levels. The <em id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">systemic</em> adversary targets global drift: a clause, exception, or interaction between clauses that the program as a whole misrepresents. It reads the full program and generates inputs on which the composition omits or misroutes part of the policy (Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Systemic adversarial examples ‣ Appendix E Adversarial examples ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>). The <em id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local</em> adversary targets local drift: a single predicate that no longer tracks the clause it encodes. It examines one predicate at a time, together with traces of the inputs that predicate receives, and generates inputs that probe where its wording diverges from the clause (Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Predicate-local adversarial examples ‣ Appendix E Adversarial examples ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>). Each sample is generated with an intended label and a justification. The teacher, applying the full policy, labels it without seeing either, and the sample enters the pool only if the two labels agree, so every admitted sample reflects two separate readings of the policy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Policy-enforcement benchmark of four tasks from real-world agentic systems. The tasks span input types, lengths, and label spaces, and each poses a distinct core challenge.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
