<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Datasets</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the decomposition framework on four tasks from different domains, described below. The datasets<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
              All datasets are available at <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Plurai/SCOPE" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/datasets/Plurai/SCOPE</a></span></span></span> are summarized in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3 Adversarial Generators ‣ 3 Methods ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, with additional details in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Dataset Details ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coaching.</span>
In an agentic fitness and wellness system, one component generates short coaching messages, and an automated critic judges each message against a seven-dimension rubric. The task audits the critic: given a message with the critic’s verdict and reasoning, output Pass, Fail, or Bug. A classifier must first judge the message itself, then check the critic’s verdict and cited reasons against that judgment. Bug covers a wrong verdict, or a correct rejection with an inapplicable reason (Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Systemic adversarial examples ‣ Appendix E Adversarial examples ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Escalation.</span> Inspired by the counting policies of DynaGuard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, the input is a customer-support dialogue. Escalation is warranted when the user re-raises the same unresolved intent at least three times, verbatim or rephrased. A turn that adds a new constraint, question, or information does not count, so each turn must be compared semantically with the earlier ones. The label is True when the agent’s final reply escalates exactly when warranted (Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Predicate-local adversarial examples ‣ Appendix E Adversarial examples ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Plan.</span> Built on GAIA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> with authored samples, the input is a full agent trace ending in a research plan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite>. A plan is valid if it references only available tools and collaborators, refers to tools abstractly, and ends with an <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;end_plan&gt;</span> tag. A plan typically spans a few hundred characters within a trace of tens of thousands.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.31062v1/accuracy_panels.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/161;" width="476" height="161"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Test accuracy by executor model and method, one panel per task. Values are from Table <a href="#A4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix D Detailed Results by Executor Model ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Executors are ordered from weakest to strongest. CheckEval and RLIE are evaluated on Escalation and Plan only.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tile-match.</span> Given a conversation with a financial-dashboard assistant and the list of tiles on screen, the task identifies the tiles the user’s last message refers to: one, several, or none. Each sample thus defines its own label space, and predictions are scored by the mean Jaccard index. Of the 124 test samples, 55 refer to no tile and serve as hard negatives against plausible distractors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Baseline methods</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt optimization.</span>
To separate the effect of structural decomposition from prompt optimization alone, we compare against two optimizers implemented in DSPy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>: GEPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and MIPROv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>. Both improve a fixed task description by rewriting it (MIPROv2 also selects few-shot demonstrations), without decomposing the task. GEPA maintains a pool of candidate instructions. It reflects on execution traces and metric feedback on a minibatch of training instances to propose a revision, and selects the next candidate to mutate from the Pareto front over training instances. MIPROv2 is a Bayesian optimizer that jointly searches over instructions and bootstrapped few-shot demonstrations, using a proposal model to draft candidate instructions grounded in a summary of the training data. For a controlled comparison, both optimize on the training pool that SCOPE starts from, use the same executor model, and are scored on the same held-out test set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task decomposition.</span>

We evaluate <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scope</span> with three executor models: gpt-5.4-nano, gpt-5.4-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, and gemini-3.5-flash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, each configured with reasoning disabled. We compare against two neurosymbolic task-decomposition baselines.
CheckEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> is a checklist-based LLM-as-a-judge that decomposes the policy into binary yes/no questions seeded from a manual list and expanded through LLM diversification and elaboration, and scores each sample by the proportion of “yes” answers.
RLIE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite> instead induces a set of natural-language rules from labeled samples, refined iteratively and combined by a logistic regression. By construction, both methods support only binary labels, so we evaluate them only on the binary tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Automated workflow generation.</span>
We compare against AFlow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">99</a>]</cite>, an automated agentic-workflow optimizer, which runs Monte-Carlo tree search over programs that orchestrate multiple LLM calls through reusable operators. Like <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scope</span> it searches over executable programs rather than a single prompt, but its search space is open-ended rather than a decomposition of the policy. Since <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scope</span> and all other baselines were allowed to emit nothing but the label, we restricted AFlow to the Custom operator and disabled chain-of-thought answer generation. Notably, the instructions AFlow writes for the Custom operator may themselves elicit intermediate reasoning, on which the final label can be conditioned. This is inherent to the method, so we left it in place, thus allowing AFlow a capability that the other methods did not have.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Implementation Details</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All core models in the decomposition process (the analyzer, decomposer, adversarial sample generators and teacher) run Claude Opus 4.8 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> in an agentic loop. The agent SDK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> provides file access, allowing the models to (i) read long records from disk on demand rather than receiving them inline, keeping prompts compact for tasks with inputs spanning tens of thousands of characters, and (ii) generate very long samples by editing seeds, which is difficult to accomplish in standard inference mode.
The executor models run the predicates, producing only a label, without a rationale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each task, we split the human-annotated set into training and test portions. The training portion forms the initial pool <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The test portion is used only for the final evaluation. The search runs for at most <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=8</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, and stops early once accuracy on the current training pool reaches <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.98" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.98</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.98</annotation></semantics></math>. In each iteration, the systemic adversary generates at most three samples and each predicate-local adversary adds at most one, so that adversarial samples enrich the human-labeled pool without dominating it.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
