<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of executor capability on the decomposition. (a) Accuracy gained when moving from the weakest to the strongest executor is substantially less for <span id="S5.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scope</span> than for prompt optimization (average gain over tasks: 0.09 vs. 0.26). (b) Number of predicates in the final decomposition (median over repetitions). Executors are ordered from weakest to strongest by prompt-optimized accuracy, an ordering that holds on all four tasks.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports test accuracy for the four tasks under the three executor models, with the exact numbers detailed in Table <a href="#A4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix D Detailed Results by Executor Model ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Detailed Results by Executor Model ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>). All reported numbers are averaged over three independent runs of the optimization process and three repetitions of the final test evaluation, for a total of 9 measurements. Both CheckEval and RLIE support binary predictions only and therefore apply to Escalation and Plan alone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing the executors on the undecomposed task after prompt optimization (maximum over GEPA and MIPROv2) reveals a consistent capability hierarchy: gpt-5.4-mini outperforms gpt-5.4-nano on all four tasks and gemini-3.5-flash outperforms gpt-5.4-mini on all four (mean accuracies over all tasks: 0.63, 0.75, 0.89).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scope</span> attains the highest accuracy, or ties for it, in 10 of the 12 task-executor cells. AFlow follows, with the highest accuracy in the remaining 2, even though it has an inherent advantage of being the only method that can ask for reasoning in intermediate steps. <span id="S5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scope</span> is also consistent across runs: with gpt-5.4-nano as the executor, its standard deviation is at most 0.03 on every task, and a quarter to a third of AFlow’s on Plan and Tile-match (Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that the gain <span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scope</span> achieves over direct prompting and prompt optimization widens as the executor weakens. This shows that the search finds decompositions that the least capable model can benefit from and execute with competitive performance. When a strong model is already able to handle the task, there is less room to improve through decompositions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Further, Figure <a href="#S5.F3.sf1" title="In Figure 3 ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a> shows that moving from the weakest executor to the strongest buys 0.26 accuracy on average under prompt optimization, but only 0.09 under <span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scope</span>. Decomposition thus compresses the capability hierarchy it was measured against: once the policy is expressed as predicates a small model can answer, accuracy becomes much less sensitive to the choice of executor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The structure of the decomposition also responds to the executor: weaker models yield more predicates (Figure <a href="#S5.F3.sf2" title="In Figure 3 ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a>), with the largest increase from gpt-5.4-mini to gpt-5.4-nano. This reflects the design of the search: when the executor cannot answer a predicate accurately, the search further splits it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, <span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scope</span> reduces to prompt optimization when decomposition does not help: the search initializes with the undecomposed task and retains the best-scoring candidate, so a single predicate is always a reachable outcome. As an example, on the coaching task with gemini-3.5-flash, the search converged to a single predicate, with test accuracy increasing from 0.87 to 0.93.</p>
</div>
