<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Cross-Executor Transferability</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The simpler the predicates, the more likely a decomposition is to transfer to other executors. We examine this by running the decompositions found using our least capable executor, gpt-5.4-nano (whose evaluation was reported in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), on five open-weight models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>, <a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>, <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>, without any additional optimization. All five models run with reasoning disabled. Compared with each model’s undecomposed run, the decompositions improve accuracy on average by roughly 0.11 on Coaching, Escalation and Plan, and 0.33 on Tile-match.
From a per-model perspective, the average gain ranges from 0.13 to 0.22 (Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the accuracy of these models running the decompositions with two high-end reasoning models evaluated directly on the undecomposed tasks: gpt-5.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> with high reasoning effort, and gemini-3.1-pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> with dynamic thinking. DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash and Qwen3-235B-A22B reach accuracy comparable to these models, at a fraction of the cost (Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Cost Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>). DeepSeek attains the highest accuracy of all models on two tasks and the second-highest on a third, while Qwen3-235B-A22B ranks second on two tasks. Both are mixture-of-experts models that activate only a fraction of their parameters for each prediction (8B/16B during input processing/generation for DeepSeek <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, and 22B for Qwen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>). The results are summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond accuracy and cost, decomposition provides a degree of stability and explainability that is absent from the monolithic high-end models. We evaluated prediction stability by running each model five times on the Tile-match dataset, whose multi-label structure requires multiple independent decisions per sample, making it particularly challenging from a stability perspective. DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash and Qwen3-235B-A22B were substantially more stable: only 5–7% of samples changed their prediction at least once across the five runs, compared with 37% for gpt-5.5 and 26% for gemini-3.1-pro. As each prediction is traceable to a set of explicit predicates, this stability yields a more explainable and interpretable decision process than the opaque predictions of the monolithic models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Ablation: Removing Adversarial Samples</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To isolate the contribution of adversarial sample generation, we disabled both adversaries, so that the training pool remains fixed at <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> throughout the search.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To achieve sufficient independent measurements for assessing statistical significance, for each setting we ran five independent optimization runs (for the Full variant, these include the three runs of Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), and averaged three test evaluations per run output. Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.2 Ablation: Removing Adversarial Samples ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> compares the resulting accuracy with and without adversarial sample generation, using gpt-5.4-nano as the executor, with significance computed by a one-sided Welch’s <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-test comparing the five measurements of the two variants. Removing adversarial sample generation reduces accuracy on Coaching from 0.839 to 0.783 (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.033" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.033</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.033</annotation></semantics></math>), on Plan from 0.865 to 0.813 (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.005</annotation></semantics></math>), and on Tile-match from 0.782 to 0.744 (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.045" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.045</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.045</annotation></semantics></math>). Adversarial sample generation thus improves accuracy on three of the four tasks, while having no effect on Escalation.</p>
</div>
