<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h3>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scope</span>, which treats a natural-language policy as a specification to compile rather than a prompt to interpret, turning it into a program of neural predicates, deterministic extractors, and composition code. The program is optimized jointly with an adversarially grown training pool. Running the predicates with a weak executor steers the search toward predicates within its reach.
Across four policy-enforcement tasks, <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scope</span> achieves the highest accuracy in most settings, with the largest gains under the weakest executor, and finds decompositions that are far less sensitive to the choice of executor.
Programs transfer to other executors without further optimization. When run by non-reasoning open-weight models, they reach accuracy comparable to frontier reasoning models, at a fraction of the cost, while producing substantially more stable verdicts, each traceable to the predicate outputs behind it.
Because the program is checked against a strong teacher rather than a formal verifier, its correctness rests on the teacher’s reading of the policy, and verifying such programs remains an open problem. The underlying optimization, which co-evolves a program with the counterexamples that challenge it, is not specific to policy enforcement and may carry over to other tasks specified in natural language, such as rubric-based LLM evaluation and reward modeling. We release our benchmark to support further research on compound policy enforcement.</p>
</div>
</section>
