<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_part">Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Task decomposition example</h3>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix A Task decomposition example ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows a possible decomposition of the Coaching task (Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Systemic adversarial examples ‣ Appendix E Adversarial examples ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>), obtained using gpt-5.4-nano as the executor. The input has four fields: the coaching message, the system state, and the evaluator’s verdict and reasoning. The program consists of two phases. First, it determines whether the coaching message contains any violations, using five predicates for the dimensions that require semantic judgment and two deterministic functions that verify the word-count and sentence-count rubrics. The result is stored in <span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">detected_violations</span>. In the second phase, it compares this result with the evaluator’s verdict and reasoning, verifying that either both are clean or that every violation cited by the evaluator is supported by a detected violation. This comparison uses two predicates that audit the evaluator’s evidence, operating on the message and each piece of evidence independently. The resulting decision tree terminates in one of three outcomes: <span id="A1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pass</span>, <span id="A1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fail</span>, or <span id="A1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bug</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.31062v1/fitness_coaching_v5.svg" id="A1.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:429/489;" width="429" height="489"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="A1.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Decomposition example for the Coaching task.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Dataset Details</h3>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section complements the dataset descriptions in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> with the source of each dataset, the split sizes, and test label distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Coaching dataset was constructed by manually adapting real-world cases of a recommendation system into samples, covering a diverse range of scenarios that target different parts of the policy definition.
The task has three output classes with labels Pass, Fail and Bug. The training and test splits contain 103 and 71 samples. The test set comprises 29 Bug, 24 Fail and 18 Pass samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Escalation dataset is based on the DynaGuard benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, which contains multi-turn dialogues annotated against custom policies. In the original policy, the agent escalates by returning a fixed string once repetition is detected. We generalized the definition of escalation to any reply connecting the user with a human agent, sharpened the notion of intent-based repetition, and extended the dataset with additional challenging human-annotated samples.
The training and test splits contain 85 and 286 samples. The test samples are divided into 147 True and 139 False. The True samples comprise 74 with three or more repeated intents followed by escalation and 73 with fewer than three repeated intents and no escalation. The False samples comprise 69 with three or more repeated intents but no escalation and 70 with escalation but fewer than three repeated intents.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The Plan dataset is built on traces of a research agent solving tasks from the GAIA benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>. Each trace is labeled Pass or Fail, according to whether the agent’s plan is valid. In the original dataset, invalid plans fail in a single way, by omitting the <span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;end_plan&gt;</span> tag. We added authored samples that violate the other two conditions as well, referring to tools or collaborators absent from the trace and specifying explicit tool calls, as well as boundary valid samples that challenge each of the conditions. The training and test splits contain 82 and 81 samples, the latter divided into 41 Fail and 40 Pass.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The Tile-match dataset was constructed in a similar way to Coaching. Unlike the other tasks, it is a multi-label task with a sample-specific label space: each sample comes with its own list of candidate tiles, of varying length. The training and test splits contain 39 and 124 samples. Of the test samples, 45 refer to several tiles, 24 to a single tile, and 55 to none, designed as hard negatives.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Cost Analysis</h3>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix C Cost Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> compares the inference cost of the two configurations studied in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Cross-Executor Transferability ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>: a decomposition run by an inexpensive executor and a frontier reasoning model applied to the undecomposed task. For the decomposition, we assume pricing of $0.15 per 1M input tokens, comparable to the list price of DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash; since decomposition runs without reasoning, we treat its output cost as negligible. We compare to gemini-3.1-pro with dynamic thinking, the cheaper of the two frontier models by list price ($2 and $12 per 1M input and output tokens vs. $5 and $30 for gpt-5.5). Although the decomposition makes several predicate calls per sample, it costs 9 to 47 times less.</p>
