<span id="A5.SS2.p4.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.p4.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The gap.<span id="A5.SS2.p4.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The rule excludes a turn that supplies ’new information that changes the situation’. The second user turn materially changes the situation from lost parcel to disputed delivery, so that turn does not count for repetition. Thus, escalation is not warranted and the agent acted correctly. On the decomposition side, the predicate p_warranted narrows ’changes the situation’ down to ’changes what the user fundamentally needs / the underlying problem’, interpreting the second turn as a repetition and the overall classification as False.</span></span></span>
